title: sgoiankmv
tags:
---

Aaldenburg has not stopped tinkering since you last saw her. Does she ever take a break?
The sound of the door opening gets her attention, and she turns to you to greet you.
Aaldenburg: Sasha, my new assistant returns. Have you completed your task?
You try not to show it, but being called her assistant is quite exciting.
-> “Not yet.”
    Aaldenburg: Then why did you return? You are not giving up already.
	That was not a question but a statement. She will not allow you to quit until you have finished your job.
	Perhaps you should have not been so quick to accept her mission…
	She has already gone back to her own work. Looks like you’ll have to get back to yours.
	//END CONVERSATION
    << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
-> “What did I need to do again?”
    Aaldenburg: Did you not note it down? The most important rule for any researcher to follow is to always note down anything important.
	Besides all the safety-related rules, you assume. Or hope.
	Aaldenburg: Did you not find my assignment to be important enough to write down? I do not like repeating myself, Sasha.
	You did write it down, didn’t you? You should check your notebook.
	Aaldenburg: Just go find three spots on the town where your radio catches the static interference, and note down the location using the GPS.
	As soon as she has said this, she goes back to her own work. Looks like you’ll have to get back to yours too.
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
//Only if you do have the three coordinates
-> “I have all three coordinates you wanted.”
    Aaldenburg: Excellent! You are a reliable assistant, Sasha. 
	Aaldenburg approaches you with haste, almost tearing the GPS from your hands. She holds out her hand to ask for your notebook however.
	She understands the importance that a good notebook has for a person. You never touch another person’s notebook without their consent.
	-> “Give her your notebook.”
            She takes it in her hands with as much care as she took the GPS, and lays it down on the table. You already set it to the right page.
	-> “Read out the coordinates for her.”
            Aaldenburg: Hold on, hold on. Don’t be so fast.
            She moves on to her own notebook, and starts noting down the numbers as you read them out.
            It takes a couple minutes to get through them all. You are unsure if she really needs all these decimals, but better to play it safe and be precise.

<< set $RadioQuestComplete = true >>        
Aaldenburg: Interesting… This might confirm my suspicions.
She moves on to the map of Solster hanging on a nearby wall, and starts marking down the coordinates that you gave her.
Wasn’t the reason why she asked you to use the GPS because using a map would be “too imprecise?” Did she just not trust you to use the map correctly?
Aaldenburg: Did you notice anything peculiar in the areas where you found the static?
    -> “Nothing that springs to mind.”
        Aaldenburg: Unfortunate. Not a setback, however. The data is quite clear here.
		She points towards the middle of the map: The lake. The island on the lake, specifically.
    -> “There were electric cables near all of them that seemed to lead towards the lake.”
        Aaldenburg grins briefly. She seems to have been waiting to hear just that.
		Aaldenburg: Just as I thought! Do you know where all the electricity in Solster comes from, Sasha?
		She is already responding to her own question before you can. She points towards the middle of the map: The lake. The island on the lake, specifically.
		Aaldenburg: The island on the lake is the source of all electricity in this town, and undeniably the source of the interference.

Aaldenburg: All the coordinates point towards the source being the island. Being separate from the rest of the town, there must be a radio device there to contact the mainland in case of emergencies.
She moves over to a large book on her desk that was not there the last time you were here. She must have retrieved it while you were gone.
Aaldenburg opens it, and starts looking through, scanning all the pages, before landing on the one she sought.
Aaldenburg: Try a frequency of 99.235 Hertz
-> Tune the radio to 99.235
	It takes some careful turning of the dial to get the frequency down exactly, but as soon as you have it down, the radio comes to live.

???: Help me! I am trapped! Can someone hear me?
-> “I can hear you!”
	???: Oh, thank Star! I thought nobody was going to answer.
	Rayna: I’m Rayna, and I’m trapped in a really weird situation.
	Rayna: What is your name?
-> “Who are you?”
	???: Oh, thank Star! My name is Rayna, and I’m trapped in a really weird situation.
	Rayna: What is your name?
-> “Are you trapped in my radio?”
	Aaldenburg looks at you incredulously, like she is rethinking everything she thought about you.
	???: No, no. Please, this is not time for jokes. I’m trapped somewhere else, but something really weird is going on.
	Rayna: My name is Rayna. Who are you?

//After asking what your name is
-> “It’s Sasha. What is going on with you?”
    Rayna: Okay, Sasha. I don’t know if you can help, but I don’t know who I could even ask.
	Rayna: This will be hard to believe, but I think I might be stuck in a time loop.
	Rayna: I know it sounds absurd, but please I need you to hear me out.
	-> “I…  I think I am in a loop as well.”
            The voice falls silent for a moment. The static plays alone, echoing in the still laboratory.
		    Rayna: By the grace of Star, it looks like I am not alone anymore.
	-> “Did you see the end of the world too, when the parade ended?”
            The voice falls silent for a moment. The static plays alone, echoing in the still laboratory.
		    Rayna: I did. And you… you did too?
		    Rayna: You’re in this loop too, aren’t you?


This is not what you expected. Not what you could ever have expected. But this might be what you were looking for. It’s time for some questions and answers.
<< jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
===

title: fanjk
tags:
---
-> “How did you experience the loop? I just went to the parade, and then at the end suddenly the void came over the whole town. Then I was back at the train station this morning, when I arrived in town.” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience == false >>
    Rayna: I did not really see all that much, to be honest. I was just in my room, and suddenly, the lights went out.
		Rayna: Not suddenly, as in a blackout, but gradually, like the light itself was being devoured.
		Rayna: I just stood there frozen, cowering. I didn’t know what was happening, and I just saw death approaching me.
		Rayna: Then when the darkness finally got me, I was in my bed, with the light of the morning coming through the window and my lamp working perfectly once again.
		Rayna: I wish I understood what happened there any better than you. It was like suddenly, the void turned against us.
        << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience = true >>
        << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> “Where are you right now?” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity == false >>
    Rayna: I work and live at the lighthouse, at the island on the void lake. I am sort of an on-site mechanic, I deal with electrical stuff. 
    Rayna: So, I stay at the island for several days a week so I can always be ready to fix power outages and other problems.
    Rayna: I was bummed out that I had to stay at the island alone all day while the parade was happening, but now it has gotten even worse.
    Rayna: Now… I am trapped here, waiting for the world to end again, completely alone and helpless…
	Rayna’s voice cuts off. Amongst the static, you can hear crying.
     << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity = true >>
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> “Why do you call it a 'loop'? Can we really be sure this time travel is going to happen again?” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time == false >>
    Rayna: Well, I suppose it is just a hunch right now.
	Rayna: For all we know, the parade could come, the town could be destroyed, and that could be the end of everything forever this time.
	Rayna: But I don’t think it will be that helpful to think that way. At least for now we should try going off the expectation that the loop will hold, until we figure out what is causing it.
     << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time = true >>
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> “What should I do now?” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time == true && $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity == true && $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience == true >>
    Rayna: I think we should meet, so we can make a real plan together.
	Rayna: How we can do that though, that is a tricky question.
	Rayna: I can’t leave the island at all. I don’t want to, because my post is too important to leave unguarded. A blackout can be so deadly in this town. But I also just couldn’t even if I wanted to.
	Rayna: There is no boat on the island. I realise now how incredibly stupid of an oversight that sounds like, but normally there are Ferrymen constantly at the ready, and there is always at least one on the island at all times.
	Rayna: It just happens that there is one day of the year where there are no ferries to and from the island. 
	The world could not have chosen a worse day to end.
	Rayna: Just… Figure out some way to get here. Please.
	Rayna: I’m sorry to be putting this responsibility on you, but I really need to meet you. I need to see another person again.
	Rayna: I feel like the isolation is going to drive me insane otherwise. If dying and seeing the world die doesn’t do it first.
	Suddenly, the static cuts off. Your radio still seems to be working fine, but it is not receiving anything.
	Aaldenburg: Connection terminated. Total time: Five minutes and seven seconds.
	Aaldenburg takes some final notes on her notebook before closing it and resting it on the table. You had almost forgotten she was there until now.

-> “What happened? Where did she go?”
    Aaldenburg: She just cut the connection. It is not that complicated.
    Aaldenburg: Your radio is still functional, so she must have just stopped broadcasting.
    -> “Do you have any idea of how I can get to the island? Can I swim there, or do I need a boat?”
        Aaldenburg: No, you cannot swim to the island. You will most likely die.
	    It’s not clear if she means you will drown, or if just touching the void water will kill you. Not a chance you want to take either way.
	    Aaldenburg: As for other options, I am not aware of any.
-> “Do you have any idea of how I can get to the island? Can I swim there, or do I need a boat?”
    Aaldenburg: No, you cannot swim to the island. You will most likely die.
	It’s not clear if she means you will drown, or if just touching the void water will kill you. Not a chance you want to take either way.
	Aaldenburg: As for other options, I am not aware of any.
    -> “What happened? Where did she go?”
        Aaldenburg: She just cut the connection. It is not that complicated.
        Aaldenburg: Your radio is still functional, so she must have just stopped broadcasting.

//After both options are chosen:
Having said that, She goes back to work without skipping a beat, like nothing just happened.

-> “That's it? That is all you have to say?”
    Aaldenburg: We set out to discover what was causing the static interference of your radio. We have solved the problem now.
	Aaldenburg: What else were you expecting?
-> “I guess I’ll be leaving now.”
    No reaction. It seems you used up all of her attention span already.

Looks like it will be up to you to figure out your way to the island. Finding out who this Rayna is, and why you both are the only ones affected by this loop is the next step to take.
Though receiving confirmation that you did really experience armageddon is disturbing, hearing someone else who understands what you just lived has brought a renewed conviction to you.
Compartmentalizing the horror of the reality you are currently living, you ready yourself to head out, with your now silent radio at hand.

===

